.TH lz6 "1" "2026-10-24" "lz6 1.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\fBlz6, unlz6, lz6cat\fR \- Compress or decompress .lz6 files

.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP 5
\fBlz6\fR [\fBOPTIONS\fR] [-|INPUT-FILE] <OUTPUT-FILE>
.PP
.B unlz6
is equivalent to
.BR "lz6 \-d" 
.br
.B lz6cat
is equivalent to
.BR "lz6 \-dc" 
.br
.PP
When writing scripts that need to decompress files,
it is recommended to always use the name
.B lz6
with appropriate arguments
.RB ( "lz6 \-d"
or
.BR "lz6 \-dc" )
instead of the names
.B unlz6
and
.BR lz6cat .


.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBlz6\fR is an extremely fast lossless compression algorithm,
based on \fBbyte-aligned LZ77\fR family of compression scheme.
\fBlz6\fR offers compression speeds of 400 MB/s per core, linearly scalable with multi-core CPUs. 
It features an extremely fast decoder, with speed in multiple GB/s per core,
typically reaching RAM speed limit on multi-core systems. 
.B lz6
supports a command line syntax similar to
.BR gzip (1).
The native file format is the
.B .lz6
format.

.SS "Concatenation of .lz6 files"
It is possible to concatenate
.B .lz6
files as is.
.B lz6
will decompress such files as if they were a single
.B .lz6
file. For example:
  lz6 file1  > foo.lz6
  lz6 file2 >> foo.lz6
 then
  lz6cat foo.lz6
 is equivalent to :
  cat file1 file2

.PP

.SH OPTIONS
.
.SS "Short commands concatenation"
In some cases, some options can be expressed using short command
.B "-x"
or long command
.B "--long-word" .
Short commands can be concatenated together. For example,
.B "-d -c"
is equivalent to
.B "-dc" .
Long commands cannot be concatenated.
They must be clearly separated by a space.
.SS "Multiple commands"
When multiple contradictory commands are issued on a same command line,
only the latest one will be applied.
.
.SS "Operation mode"
.TP
.BR \-\-hc
Compress with the classic LZ6-HC frame codec (hash chains + binary
tree, levels 1-15, a scale of its own that did not change) instead of the seq engine. Decompression is
codec-agnostic: the frame magic selects the block coder automatically.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-compress
Compress.
This is the default operation mode 
when no operation mode option is specified ,
no other operation mode is implied from the command name 
(for example,
.B unlz6
implies
.B \-\-decompress ),
nor from the input file name 
(for example, a file extension
.B .lz6
implies
.B \-\-decompress
by default).
.B -z
can also be used to force compression of an already compressed
.B .lz6
file.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decompress ", " \-\-uncompress
Decompress.
.B --decompress
is also the default operation when the input filename has an
.B .lz6
extensionq
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test
Test the integrity of compressed
.B .lz6
files.
The decompressed data is discarded.
No files are created nor removed.
A file that is not an lz6 stream is an error, with or without
.B \-f
(the pass-through of
.B \-d \-f
does not apply to a test).
.
.SS "Operation modifiers"
.TP
.B \-0
 fast compression with the classic LZ frame codec (default)
.TP
.B \-1 ... \-13
 the seq engine. \-1 compresses like the first level of the LZMA family (about 28.3% on the Silesia corpus) at several times
 their speed; a higher number compresses more and is slower; \-7 is a middle level. 14 and 15 are accepted and mean 13.
 Before v1.6.12-pre the scale was 1-15 and \-1 and \-2 were the fast tier: the old \-N (N >= 3) is \-(N-2) now.
.TP
.B \--fast[=N]
 the seq engine's fast tier, for speed over ratio: N = 1 (default, about 30.5%, 110 MB/s encode) or N = 2 (about 32.6%,
 140 MB/s); a larger N is faster. The old \-2 and \-1.

.TP
.BR \-f ", " --[no-]force
 This option has several effects:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
If the target file already exists,
overwrite it without prompting.
.IP \(bu 3
When used with
.B \-\-decompress
and
.B lz6
cannot recognize the type of the source file,
copy the source file as is to standard output.
This allows
.B lz6cat
.B \-\-force
to be used like
.BR cat (1)
for files that have not been compressed with
.BR lz6 .
.RE

.TP
.BR \-c ", " \--stdout ", " \--to-stdout
 force write to standard output, even if it is the console

.TP
.BR \-m ", " \--multiple
 Multiple file names.
 By default, the second filename is used as the output filename for the compressed file.
 With
.B -m
, you can specify any number of input filenames. Each of them will be compressed
independently, and the resulting name of the compressed file will be
.B filename.lz6
.

.TP
.B \-B#
 block size, 1 to 7: B1= 64KB ; B2= 256KB ; B3= 1MB ; B4= 4MB ; B5= 16MB (the default) ; B6= 64MB ; B7= 256MB.
 A file larger than 16 MB gets 7 by default (6 with
.B --hc ).
 A number outside 1-7 is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-T#
 worker threads (default : 1; 0 = one per CPU core).
 Compression uses them for the seq engine (\-1 to \-13 and \-\-fast); with
.B --hc
or
.B -0
it runs on one thread. Decompression uses them for any file.
 The compressed file is byte-for-byte the same at any thread count, and any
 file can be decompressed with any thread count. Each thread works on one block,
 so memory grows with both
.B -B
and
.B -T
(about two blocks per thread, plus the compressor's tables), and a file of a single
block does not speed up. Frames with linked blocks are always decoded on one thread.
.TP
.B \--[no-]frame-crc
 select frame checksum (default:enabled)
.TP
.B \--[no-]content-size
 header includes original size (default:not present)
 Note : this option can only be activated when the original size can be determined,
hence for a file. It won't work with unknown source size, such as stdin or pipe.
.TP
.B \--[no-]sparse 
 sparse mode support (default:enabled on file, disabled on stdout)
.
.SS "Other options"
.TP
.BR \-v ", " --verbose
 verbose mode
.TP
.BR \-q ", " --quiet
 suppress warnings; specify twice to suppress errors too
.TP
.B \-h/\-H
 display help/long help and exit
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \--version
 display Version number and exit
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \--keep
 Don't delete source file.
This is default behavior anyway, so this option is just for compatibility with gzip/xz.
.TP
.B \-b
 benchmark file(s)
.TP
.B \-i#
 iteration loops [1-9](default : 3), benchmark mode only

.SH "END OF OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \--
 Everything after it is a file name, even if it starts with a dash.

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success. Non-zero on any error; the status is the number printed in
.B "Error N : ..."
. The ones that matter in scripts: 1 usage error or unreadable input file (a directory is refused),
11 the output file exists and cannot be overwritten (no
.B \-f ,
no console to ask on, or the prompt got the end of the input),
12 the answer to the overwrite prompt was not yes (Enter and
.B y
mean yes),
44 the input is not an lz6 stream,
48 a read failed (the output is not complete),
66 the stream is corrupt (the checksum or the data), 67 it is truncated.
Without
.B \-f
an existing output is never overwritten unasked.

